::GEO_U04_PT_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A transformation sequence says reflect across the y-axis, then dilate by a scale factor of 2. Which step happens first? {
=A. Reflect across the y-axis#The sequence is read in order, so the reflection happens before the dilation.
~B. Dilate by a scale factor of 2#This reverses the stated sequence.
~C. Rotate 90&deg;#A rotation is not included in the stated sequence.
~D. Translate right 2 units#A translation is not included in the stated sequence.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure has a vertical line that divides it into matching mirror halves. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Rotation symmetry#Rotation symmetry involves turning a figure onto itself, not mirror halves across a line.
=B. Reflection symmetry#A line that creates matching mirror halves shows reflection symmetry.
~C. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry involves sliding a repeated figure.
~D. Dilation symmetry#A dilation changes size and does not describe mirror halves.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation can justify that two figures are similar but not necessarily congruent? {
~A. Translation#A translation preserves size, so it supports congruence rather than non-congruent similarity.
~B. Reflection#A reflection preserves size, so it supports congruence rather than non-congruent similarity.
=C. Dilation#A dilation changes size by a scale factor while preserving shape, so it supports similarity.
~D. Rotation#A rotation preserves size, so it supports congruence rather than non-congruent similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which similarity criterion is supported? {
~A. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires three pairs of proportional corresponding sides.
~B. SAS similarity#SAS similarity requires two proportional side pairs and a congruent included angle.
~C. HL congruence#HL is a right-triangle congruence criterion, not a triangle similarity criterion.
=D. AA similarity#Two pairs of congruent corresponding angles are enough to justify AA similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar polygons have corresponding side lengths 6 and 18. What is the scale factor from the smaller polygon to the larger polygon? {
=A. 3#The scale factor is 18 divided by 6, which equals 3.
~B. 12#This subtracts the side lengths instead of comparing them multiplicatively.
~C. &frac13;#This is the reverse scale factor from the larger polygon to the smaller polygon.
~D. 24#This adds the side lengths and does not describe scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines tangent? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This ratio defines sine, not tangent.
=B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~C. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This ratio defines cosine, not tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This reverses a sine relationship and is not tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 23#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=C. 17#Use 8<sup>2</sup> + 15<sup>2</sup> = 289, so the hypotenuse is 17.
~D. 120#Multiplying the legs gives area-like information, not the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model uses a scale factor of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> from the actual object to the model. An actual length is 40 cm. What is the model length? {
~A. 35 cm#Subtracting 5 does not apply a scale factor.
~B. 45 cm#Adding 5 does not apply a scale factor.
~C. 200 cm#This uses the reciprocal scale factor and scales up instead of down.
=D. 8 cm#Multiply 40 by <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> to get 8 cm.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>The side lengths of one triangle are 4, 5, and 6. The corresponding side lengths of another triangle are 8, 10, and 12. Which conclusion is supported? {
=A. The triangles are similar by SSS similarity.#Each corresponding side ratio is 2, so all three side pairs are proportional.
~B. The triangles are congruent by SSS congruence.#The side lengths are proportional but not equal, so congruence is not supported.
~C. The triangles are similar by AA similarity.#No angle information is given, so AA similarity is not the supported criterion.
~D. No similarity conclusion is possible.#A similarity conclusion is possible because all corresponding side ratios are equal.
}

::GEO_U04_PT_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_PT_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder is 13 ft long and its base is 5 ft from a wall. How high does the ladder reach? {
~A. 18 ft#Adding the ladder length and base distance does not use the right-triangle relationship.
=B. 12 ft#The ladder is the hypotenuse, so h<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> = 13<sup>2</sup>, giving h = 12 ft.
~C. 8 ft#Subtracting 5 from 13 ignores the squared side relationship.
~D. &radic;194 ft#This adds the squares even though 13 is already the hypotenuse.
}
